{{Japanese name|Hira}}

'''[[Toki Hira]]''' ({{nihongo|比良 時|Hira Toki}}; 5 April 1848 – 22 December 1913) was a Sanese politician and bureaucrat who served as the 6th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] for two non-consecutive terms: first from July 1903 to January 1904, and again from November 1905 to April 1907. A non-party, bureaucratic prime minister appointed as a neutral choice after [[Juichi Miyazaki]]'s assassination, Hira's first government was short-lived, failing to manage the growing tensions with [[Illypnian]] colonial powers over [[Shendan]] trade routes. His second term, a "government of national unity," focused on military expansion and managing the Shendan maritime economy following the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding]].

==Early life and education==
[[Toki Hira]] was born in [[Hirogawa Prefecture|Hirogawa]] in 1848, the son of a rural physician who had adopted Sanese nationality after the [[Saito Restoration]]. Hira's family was of modest means, and his rise to the premiership was a testament to the meritocratic ideals of the early Saito state, which promoted talent over lineage.

Hira attended the prefectural school in Hirogawa before winning a scholarship to [[Okami Imperial University]], where he studied law and public administration. He graduated in 1871 and entered the civil service, one of the first generation of career bureaucrats who would come to be known as the "bureaucratic elite" of the Saito state.

==Political career==
Hira rose steadily through the ranks of the Home Ministry, becoming known as a competent administrator with a particular talent for managing the complex relationship between the central government and the prefectural authorities. He was appointed vice-minister of the Home Ministry in 1888 and [[Minister of the Interior (Okaiken)|Minister of the Interior]] in 1896.

As Interior Minister, Hira oversaw the implementation of the local government reforms that standardized the prefectural system and extended central administrative control to the remote northern and southern provinces. He also managed the government's response to the peasant uprisings of 1897, which had been triggered by the heavy land tax burden.

Hira was not a political partisan. He belonged to no faction within the ruling establishment and was widely respected for his administrative impartiality. This reputation made him a natural compromise candidate when the political system was paralyzed by factional conflict.

==First Premiership (1903–1904)==
[[File:Hira Constitutional Crisis 1903.png|thumb|right|200px|The constitutional crisis between the non-party bureaucracy and the Rikken Seiyūkai]]
Hira was appointed Prime Minister on 16 July 1903, following the assassination of [[Juichi Miyazaki]] on 12 October 1902. The intervening period had seen [[Kinmochi Saionji]] serve as acting prime minister, but the genrō council was unable to agree on a permanent successor. Hira was chosen as a "neutral" figure who could govern without favoring any particular faction.

The Hira government's primary challenge was the growing tension with [[Illypnian]] colonial powers over [[Shendan]] trade routes. The Illypnian powers—particularly Volisania and Breisland—had been expanding their colonial presence in the Shendan region, imposing restrictive trade regulations that threatened Sanese commercial interests. Hira attempted to negotiate a resolution through diplomatic channels, but the Illypnian powers refused to make concessions.

Domestically, Hira's government was weakened by its lack of a parliamentary base. The Rikken Seiyūkai, which had been founded by Miyazaki, controlled the Diet and was increasingly assertive in demanding a role in government. Hira's refusal to appoint Seiyūkai members to his cabinet led to a series of budget disputes that paralyzed the government.

Hira resigned on 11 January 1904, after only six months in office, having failed to manage either the foreign policy crisis or the domestic political deadlock. He was succeeded by [[Kinmochi Saionji]], who took leadership of the Rikken Seiyūkai and formed the first party-based government since Koyanagi's brief experiment.

Despite its brevity, the first Hira government achieved several significant domestic reforms. The Constitutional Crisis of 1903, precipitated by the Diet's refusal to pass the government's budget, established the important precedent that the House of Commoners possessed the right to amend budget legislation—a precedent that would strengthen the Diet's position in subsequent decades. Hira also introduced the Election Reform Act of 1903, which reduced the property qualification for voting and expanded the electorate by approximately 15%, though it fell short of the universal suffrage demanded by the reformist parties.

In foreign policy, the first Hira government's failure to resolve the Shendan trade disputes had lasting consequences. The Illypnian powers' refusal to negotiate convinced a growing faction within the military that diplomatic engagement was futile and that Okaiken's interests in the Shendan could only be secured through military force. This conviction would shape the strategic debates of the following decade.

==Second Premiership (1905–1907)==
Hira returned to power on 2 November 1905, following the resignation of Saionji's first government amid public dissatisfaction over foreign policy. This time, Hira was appointed to lead a "government of national unity" that would include both party politicians and non-party bureaucrats.

The second Hira government focused on two main priorities: military expansion and the management of the Shendan maritime economy. The [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding]], signed during the Taku administration, had created new opportunities for Sanese trade in the Shendan region, but it had also heightened tensions with the Illypnian colonial powers, who viewed the treaty as a challenge to their dominance.

Hira's government authorized a significant expansion of the Imperial Sanese Navy, 
[[File:Hira Naval Expansion 1906.png|thumb|right|200px|Naval expansion authorized during Hira's second term, including six new battleships]]
including the construction of six new battleships and the establishment of a permanent naval squadron in the Shendan. The government also invested heavily in the merchant marine, providing subsidies for Sanese shipping companies that operated in the Shendan trade routes.

On the domestic front, Hira's government passed the National Railway Expansion Act of 1906, which authorized the construction of 2,000 kilometers of new railway lines to support the growing industrial economy. The act was funded through a combination of government bonds and a modest increase in the land tax.

Despite these achievements, Hira's government was ultimately unable to resolve the fundamental tension between the party-based Diet and the non-party bureaucracy. The Rikken Seiyūkai continued to demand greater influence over cabinet appointments, and Hira's attempts to maintain a "national unity" balance became increasingly untenable. He resigned on 18 April 1907, and was succeeded by [[Kinmochi Saionji]], who returned for a second term.

On the foreign policy front, the second Hira government's naval expansion and Shendan maritime policy had significant long-term consequences. The government negotiated the Shendan Maritime Access Agreement of 1906, which secured Sanese commercial vessels the right of innocent passage through the key straits controlled by Illypnian colonial authorities—an agreement that reduced, though did not eliminate, the trade restrictions that had plagued the first Hira government. The agreement was Hira's most significant diplomatic achievement, and it provided a model for the Shendan commercial treaty network that [[Masaharu Ashikaga]] would later expand.

The government also deepened Okaiken's involvement in [[Kwangju]] affairs. Building on the Resident-General system established under Miyazaki, Hira authorized increased economic cooperation with the Kwangju government, including the construction of railways and the development of mining concessions. These activities, while commercially beneficial, heightened tensions with Shansi, which viewed growing Sanese influence in Kwangju as a threat to its own continental interests.

==Legacy==
Toki Hira is remembered as a competent administrator who was thrust into the premiership by political circumstances rather than by personal ambition. His two terms illustrate the fundamental instability of the Saito constitutional order, which could not reconcile the demands of party-based democracy with the tradition of bureaucratic governance.

His second term's focus on naval expansion and Shendan maritime policy had lasting consequences, laying the groundwork for Okaiken's emergence as the dominant naval power in the Shendan region—a position that would shape the nation's strategic posture for the next half-century.

After his resignation, Hira retired from active politics and returned to private life. He died in [[Okami]] on 22 December 1913 at the age of 65.

